export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>Throughout the 1980s, the company behind Mario was the most dominant video game maker on the market, producing hit after hit, with the highlight being <em>Super Mario Bros. 3</em> (1988) reaching the \"bestselling game in history\" status at the time. The year 1988 was the same year that their competitor, the developer, released their third home console, the Genesis. The company's initial approach to their new hardware was simply porting their arcade titles to it, instead of generating new ones. However, the resounding success of Mario compelled them into trying the same approach.</p>\n<p>As a result, the developer began incubating a new game idea, something that would demonstrate the power of the Genesis, complete with a star character that could go head to head against Mario. As the new decade arrived, the developer was still playing from behind. And because of that, they gotta go fast.</p>\n<p>In 1991, Sonic dashed into public awareness with <em>Sonic The Hedgehog</em>.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/b7140b5b49180a8417d9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/b7140b5b49180a8417d9.webp\" width=\"630\" height=\"352\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td>\n</td><td><p>The idea for Sonic didn't come up on a whim. Sometime in 1990, the developer organized an internal contest in hopes of finding their new flagship title. Their employees grouped up and started conceptualizing their own games. Among those employees, there were an artist and a programmer.</p>\n<p>In order to showcase the rendering capacity of the Genesis, a programmer built a tech demo that allowed a 2D sprite to coast along curved surfaces smoothly, opening the door for a platformer that emphasizes blistering speed and long-winding structures. the developer was intrigued by the concept, and thus, greenlit the project.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Since the gameplay was figured out, their focus then centered around designing the player character, a potential mascot for the company. The first animal they tried out was not a hedgehog, but a rabbit with large ears that could grab objects in the level. Though much testing proved to them that this concept was too complex to materialize on the Genesis.</p>\n<p>After abandoning the rabbit concept, the team reconsidered their gameplay and realized they could use an animal that could transform into a ball to take advantage of the curved geometry of their levels. Eventually they settled on Sonic, a stylish  anthropomorphic hedgehog. In terms of color, Sonic's cobalt blue followed the cobalt blue in the company’s logo while his shoes drew inspiration from an album <em>cover</em>.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/4ba66426fcc1e99513cd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/4ba66426fcc1e99513cd.webp\" width=\"512\" height=\"300\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/59264b561be974dbcf4d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/59264b561be974dbcf4d.webp\" width=\"630\" height=\"352\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Sonic Mania (2017)</em></p></td><td><p>On the surface level, Mario and Sonic share a lot of similarities in terms of gameplay. In essence, they both revolve around pushing forward, vaulting over obstacles, and stomping enemies. However, whereas Mario focuses on precise jumping, Sonic is all about maintaining top speed.</p>\n<p>This difference in focus is most apparent in the level design. Mario's levels often progress in one direction, with the primary challenge being the width of pitfalls and their spacing. Meanwhile, the levels in Sonic are comparatively more sprawling, and the progression paths not only unfold on the horizontal axis but also vertically.</p>\n<p>In short, Mario's levels demand precise execution of the jump mechanic while Sonic's levels demand lightning-fast reflexes and efficient routing.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Fluidity in motion and expansive level design, compounded with a confident and sleek player character, proved a winning formula for the developer. Early <em>Sonic</em> games turned out wildly successful as they had hoped, even surpassing Mario in terms of popularity during his prime.</p>\n<div class=\"source-panel\"></div>\n<p>Looking back, playing from behind wasn't necessarily a bad thing for the developer. In fact, it allowed them to retrospectively learn from and improve upon existing ideas. That said, it would be extra hard to play catch-up if you didn't happen to have supersonic speed.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/c4ce2f142b8091ff4ee4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/c4ce2f142b8091ff4ee4.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"575\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Let's forget Sonic's initial design for the live-action film. </em><em></em></p></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
export const images = ["assets/cookies-inline/4ba66426fcc1e99513cd.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/59264b561be974dbcf4d.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/b7140b5b49180a8417d9.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/c4ce2f142b8091ff4ee4.webp"];
